  "prediction": "**Summary of the Report: Implementation of CAPTA Requirements for Substance-Affected Infants**\n\nThe report examines the implementation of the Child Abuse Prevention and Treatment Act (CAPTA) requirements for substance-affected infants. CAPTA requires states to have policies and procedures in place to address the needs of infants born with prenatal substance exposure. The report highlights the challenges states face in implementing these requirements, including a lack of clarity on key terms and a need for additional guidance and technical assistance.\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n*   42 states require health care providers to notify Child Protective Services (CPS) about substance-affected infants, but the circumstances under which notification is required vary.\n*   49 states have policies and procedures for deciding which notifications about substance-affected infants are accepted for investigation.\n*   38 states reported that additional guidance on requirements for health care providers to notify CPS about substance-affected infants would be extremely or very helpful.\n*   37 states reported that additional guidance on developing, implementing, and monitoring plans of safe care for substance-affected infants would be extremely or very helpful.\n\n**Challenges:**\n\n*   States struggle with defining key terms, such as \"substance-affected\" and \"plan of safe care.\"\n*   States face challenges in implementing new CAPTA requirements, including a lack of clarity on what is required and how to develop plans of safe care.\n*   States need additional guidance and technical assistance to address these challenges.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n\nThe Government Accountability Office (GAO) recommends that the Secretary of Health and Human Services (HHS) direct the Administration for Children and Families (ACF) to provide additional guidance and technical assistance to states to address known challenges and enhance their understanding of CAPTA requirements.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nThe report highlights the importance of providing adequate supports and services to substance-affected infants and their families. Effective implementation of CAPTA requirements is critical to ensuring the safety and well-being of these vulnerable infants. Additional guidance and technical assistance from HHS can help states better understand and implement these requirements, ultimately leading to improved outcomes for children and families.",
